course: dictionary
lesson: 12051
title: A word for the whole people
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Citizenry is a formal word for all the citizens of a country, town or place, thought of as one group. A citizen is one person who belongs to a place, so the citizenry is the whole group of them taken together.
explain: Look at the clues in «an informed citizenry». The word after citizenry tells you what the whole group is like: informed means they know a lot about what is going on. Try swapping in "the people" and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: You will meet citizenry in history, politics and news writing, such as «the citizenry of Athens». It tells the reader we mean the whole people, not one person. In everyday English we usually say "the people" or "citizens" instead.
fact: Citizenry means all the citizens of a country, town or place, thought of as one group.
fact: Citizenry is a formal word, so you will mostly see it in history, politics and news writing.
fact: «The citizenry of Athens» means all the people of the city of Athens, taken together.
fact: «An informed citizenry» means a whole population that knows what is going on.
fact: A citizen is one person who belongs to a country or town; citizenry is the whole group of them.
exercise: «The citizenry of Athens» means | One ruler who governed the city | The old buildings of the city | *All the citizens of Athens, as one group | A single visitor to the city
exercise: «An informed citizenry» means | One person who loves watching the news | *A whole group of people who know a lot about what is going on | A group of people who cannot read at all
exercise: In «the citizenry of the town», which everyday word could replace citizenry? | the weather | the trees | the roads | *the people
test: «The citizenry voted in the election.» What does citizenry mean here? | One single citizen who lives in the town | A ruler who makes the laws | A set of written rules | *All the citizens, as one group
test: Which everyday words are a plain way of saying citizenry? | *the people and citizens | the tools and machines | the animals and plants | the buildings and roads
exam: Sam says citizenry means one person who lives in a town. What is the correct meaning? | A written list of the town's laws | A place where many people meet | One single person who lives in a town | *A group of all the citizens, taken together
